N2  - Summary The magnetosheath is a spatially-bounded transition region between the bow shock and the magnetopause. It is formed when the solar wind plasma encounters the bow shock and passes by the magnetosphere of a planetary body. Serving as the direct interface to the magnetospheric plasma, lessons on the magnetosheath and the plasma processes therein have immediate impacts on our understanding of the magnetospheric dynamics. Our knowledge of the magnetosheath is rapidly expanding thanks to multispacecraft missions with the spatial resolution, development in the measurement techniques and analysis tools, novel theoretical models, and availability of high-performance computing facilities for global numerical simulations. A review is given on the recent progress in the Earth magnetosheath studies, in particular, large-scale structure, waves and turbulence, and transient events. Also, some of the unsolved problems and the possible future research directions are highlighted based on the recent advances in the magnetosheath research.
