N2  - Summary This chapter describes the different types of galaxies and the factors that may determine various morphological features. Galaxy morphology and classification began with simple descriptive terms for angular size, brightness and central concentration based on visual observations. The chapter also describes the different classes of galaxies within the framework of the ?Comprehensive de Vaucouleurs revised Hubble?Sandage? classification system, a visual system that follows the precepts of de Vaucouleurs, who proposed a personal revision of the Hubble?Sandage classification that he believed to provide a better description of galaxies without being too unwieldy. Hubble recognized that there are basically two classes of galaxies: disk-shaped galaxies and non-disk-shaped galaxies. Elliptical galaxies are characterized by a well-defined interconnectedness of their physical parameters. Spiral galaxies are generally two-component systems consisting of a bulge and a disk. S0 galaxies are generally viewed as ?armless? disk galaxies.
